          },
          "mechanism": "Age is a strong independent predictor of hypertension diagnosis (prevalence increases with age) and is associated with insurance type availability and enrollment patterns (age-based eligibility and costs).",
          "justification": "Age is independently associated with both hypertension prevalence and insurance eligibility patterns.",
          "role": "confounder",
          "proxy_for": null,
          "contestable": false
        },
        {
          "variable": {
            "kind": "variable",
            "key": "m1:Q3.3",
            "quoted_wording": "sha256:1502a55645513419f8997fa1a3261928116635487d10c83882c7b7ce91cc61fa"
          },
          "mechanism": "Biological sex is associated with hypertension risk (male sex has higher prevalence at younger ages) and may be associated with insurance patterns through occupational or family composition pathways.",
          "justification": "Sex is independently associated with both hypertension risk and insurance patterns.",
          "role": "confounder",
          "proxy_for": null,
          "contestable": false
        },
        {
          "variable": {
            "kind": "variable",
            "key": "m1:Q3.4",
            "quoted_wording": "sha256:f3dbc7a30fdb98096b1da4d67cd54ba49a56884dd3ad168d59ad41d703fb4884"
          },
          "mechanism": "Gender identity may be associated with socioeconomic factors and healthcare utilization patterns that affect both insurance type and healthcare access for hypertension screening.",
          "justification": "Gender identity is associated with socioeconomic factors and healthcare utilization patterns affecting insurance and screening access.",
          "role": "precision",
          "proxy_for": null,
          "contestable": false
        },
        {
          "variable": {
            "kind": "variable",
            "key": "m1:Q3.10",
            "quoted_wording": "sha256:780728bf35035558d044eb83b0a0913fa9ac23432800ba7771332210ddb7cedd"
          },
          "mechanism": "Race/ethnicity is associated with hypertension prevalence (substantial health disparities documented) and with insurance coverage patterns (differential enrollment rates and coverage types by race).",
          "justification": "Race/ethnicity is independently associated with both hypertension prevalence and insurance coverage patterns.",
          "role": "confounder",
          "proxy_for": null,
          "contestable": false
        },
        {
          "variable": {
            "kind": "variable",
            "key": "m1:Q3.8",
            "quoted_wording": "sha256:a10a496fc05ed9ee27e352d585b73564c6380fa93220d96d5bb1bf12b34e95e6"
          },
          "mechanism": "Hispanic/Latino ethnicity is independently associated with insurance coverage (differential access to employer-based coverage) and with hypertension diagnosis patterns.",
          "justification": "Hispanic/Latino ethnicity is independently associated with both insurance coverage and hypertension diagnosis patterns.",
          "role": "confounder",
          "proxy_for": null,
          "contestable": false
        },
        {
          "variable": {
            "kind": "variable",
            "key": "m1:Q3.11",
            "quoted_wording": "sha256:45f00b152c1b85953fc15fb60065f1ca1ffffc566a72d7902ecca4f602786ef1"
          },
          "mechanism": "Education is a fundamental component of socioeconomic status, associated with both insurance type (higher education predicts better coverage) and hypertension risk (through health literacy, occupational exposure, and behavioral pathways).",
          "justification": "Education is a fundamental SES indicator associated with both insurance type and hypertension risk.",
          "role": "confounder",
          "proxy_for": null,
          "contestable": false
        },
        {
          "variable": {
            "kind": "variable",
            "key": "m1:Q5.4",
            "quoted_wording": "sha256:79590ca38c808b483369e51527ceca6aca100f2472a404c51b46d36b4411c743"
          },
          "mechanism": "Income is the primary determinant of insurance type and affordability; it is also a fundamental social determinant of hypertension risk through access to healthy food, safe neighborhoods, stress, and healthcare utilization.",
          "justification": "Income is the primary determinant of insurance type and a fundamental social determinant of hypertension risk.",
          "role": "confounder",
          "proxy_for": null,
          "contestable": false
        },
        {
          "variable": {
            "kind": "variable",
            "key": "m1:Q2.10",
